    "slug": "home-based-teaching-initiative-in-hargeisa-opens-doors-for-out-of-school-learners",
    "summary": "In Hargeisa, a group of young teachers is offering home schooling to children and adults who cannot attend formal schools due to financial or access barriers, providing both education and income.",
    "content": "In Hargeisa, the capital of Somaliland, a new educational approach is taking shape outside traditional classrooms. Asma Ismail Ahmed, a 23-year-old teacher, spends her afternoons visiting homes to instruct children and adults who have been excluded from formal schooling. The initiative, which began in May, involves eight young educators who target densely populated neighborhoods where schools are scarce.\n\nAsma and her colleagues charge families between $5 and $10 per month, making lessons affordable for many who otherwise could not afford education. For Asma, the work serves a dual purpose: it provides a steady income while filling a critical gap in the city's educational infrastructure. Many families in these areas have long faced financial constraints or a lack of nearby schools, leaving them without access to basic learning.\n\nThe home-schooling model responds to a growing demand for flexible education in Hargeisa, where rapid urbanization has strained existing school capacity. By bringing lessons directly to students, the teachers circumvent the need for expensive infrastructure and allow learners to study at their own pace within their own homes. This approach particularly benefits women and girls, who may face cultural or security barriers to attending formal schools outside their neighborhoods.\n\nFor the Somali diaspora, this initiative highlights resilience and innovation in the homeland's education sector. Many diaspora members send remittances to support relatives' schooling, and home schooling offers a cost-effective alternative that can complement existing support. It also serves as a reminder of the challenges facing education in Somaliland, where many children remain out of school despite government and international efforts.\n\nAs word spreads, the program's success could inspire similar efforts in other Somali cities, offering a template for community-led educational solutions. While formal systems remain the ultimate goal, home schooling provides an immediate, practical remedy for those left behind, ensuring that learning continues in the face of adversity.\n\nRead the original report from Radio Ergo here: https://radioergo.org/en/2026/08/home-schooling-in-hargeisa-expands-opportunity-for-education/",
