    "id": "news-1786948466113-2",
    "title": "Somalia Pilots New Oral Polio Vaccine Campaign in Mogadishu",
    "original_title": "Soomaaliya oo Tijaabisay Hannaan Cusub oo Tallaalka Dabaysha lagu Gaarsiinayo Carruurta Muqdisho",
    "slug": "somalia-pilots-new-oral-polio-vaccine-campaign-in-mogadishu",
    "summary": "Somalia has launched a pilot campaign to administer the novel oral polio vaccine type 2 (nOPV2) to over 122,000 children in three high-risk districts of Mogadishu. The initiative, supported by the World Health Organization, targets children under five in Daynile, Garasbaley, and Kahda.",
    "content": "In mid-August 2026, Somali health authorities, with support from the World Health Organization (WHO), introduced a new method for delivering polio vaccines to children in high-risk areas of the capital. The pilot campaign ran for five days, during which 122,067 children under the age of five received the novel oral polio vaccine type 2 (nOPV2) in three districts of the Banaadir region: Daynile, Garasbaley, and Kahda. These districts were identified as having elevated risk for poliovirus transmission.\n\nThe nOPV2 vaccine is a newer formulation designed to reduce the risk of vaccine-derived polio outbreaks, which have been a challenge in areas with low routine immunization coverage. The campaign aimed to reach 94% of the target population in these districts, marking a significant step in Somalia's ongoing efforts to eradicate polio. The choice of these districts reflects their dense populations and previous gaps in vaccination coverage.\n\nThis pilot is part of a broader strategy by the WHO and Somalia's Ministry of Health to stop the spread of polio, which has re-emerged in several regions due to conflict and limited healthcare access. The use of nOPV2 is seen as a more stable and safer option compared to older oral vaccines. The campaign involved community health workers who went door-to-door to ensure children were vaccinated, particularly in areas where access to health facilities is limited.\n\nFor the Somali diaspora, this development is significant as it demonstrates progress in public health infrastructure in Somalia, which can encourage investment and support for health initiatives. It also reassures diaspora families planning to visit that efforts are being made to curb preventable diseases. The success of this pilot could lead to broader vaccination campaigns across the country, reducing the risk of polio for all Somalis.\n\nRead the original report from Goobjoog here: https://goobjoog.com/2026/08/17/soomaaliya-oo-tijaabisay-hannaan-cusub-oo-tallaalka-dabaysha-lagu-gaarsiinayo-carruurta-muqdisho/",
