    "original_title": "Maxkamad Maraykan ah oo oggolaatay Masaafurinta ku dhowaad 1,100 Qof oo Soomaali ah",
    "slug": "us-court-allows-termination-of-tps-for-nearly-1-100-somalis",
    "summary": "A US federal court has permitted the Trump administration to end Temporary Protected Status (TPS) for approximately 1,100 Somali nationals, with the designation officially ending on August 14, 2026.",
    "content": "A federal court in the United States has cleared the way for the Trump administration to terminate Temporary Protected Status (TPS) for nearly 1,100 Somali nationals. The decision, handed down this week, removes a legal barrier that had temporarily blocked the termination, allowing the Department of Homeland Security to proceed with ending the protection.\n\nTPS is a temporary immigration status granted to nationals from countries experiencing armed conflict, natural disasters, or other extraordinary conditions. It permits beneficiaries to reside and work lawfully in the US without the immediate threat of deportation. For Somali nationals, TPS has been a critical lifeline, as many fled decades of conflict and instability in Somalia.\n\nThe US Citizenship and Immigration Services (USCIS) has confirmed that the TPS designation for Somalia officially ended on August 14, 2026. This means that affected individuals will lose their legal status and work authorization, potentially facing deportation unless they obtain another form of legal relief.\n\nThe court's ruling follows a series of legal challenges mounted by advocacy groups and affected individuals, who argued that the termination was premature given the continuing volatile security situation in Somalia. However, the court sided with the administration, citing the government's authority to review and end such designations as circumstances change.\n\nFor the Somali diaspora, this development carries significant implications. Many TPS holders have built lives in the US over years, with families, jobs, and community ties. The loss of status could disrupt these communities and place individuals in legal limbo. Advocacy organizations are urging affected Somalis to explore options such as asylum, family-based petitions, or other forms of humanitarian relief, and to seek legal counsel promptly.\n\nRead the original report from Goobjoog here: https://goobjoog.com/2026/08/17/maxkamad-maraykan-ah-oo-oggolaatay-masaafurinta-ku-dhowaad-1100-qof-oo-soomaali-ah/",
